WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:13.680
The entire premise of our republic rests on
an informed citizenry, Americans need to understand

00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:18.180
what their government is doing at all the
different levels. Only journalists provide

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:21.430
that service in the interest of the truth.

00:00:21.430 --> 00:00:25.290
You can't have freedom without knowledge and
education of what's going on,

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:27.120
and that's what the journalist's job is.

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:31.180
A journalist without integrity can always
find the story he's looking for. Responsible

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:37.399
journalism has to have a broader perspective,
has to have enough of an education to say,

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:41.620
"What we really ought to be looking at is
not what everyone says the story is."

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:52.280
We have a very big, ambitious core curriculum,
and that means that everyone here gets fundamentally

00:00:52.289 --> 00:00:56.729
educated. They study the great subjects, they
highest subjects about the highest things.

00:00:56.729 --> 00:01:01.449
That gives them tools, first of all, to know
what good writing is, but second, to know

00:01:01.449 --> 00:01:07.000
how things that are going on today fit in
the story of the world. We have kids from

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:11.430
every discipline here who are very equipped
to become journalists.

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:16.120
I think Hillsdale provides you with a really
cohesive curriculum. As a journalist, that

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:20.580
helped me become a critical thinker. It helps
me come up with what good questions are.

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:24.420
The Liberal Arts gives you a wide breadth of knowledge
and you kind of get to see the world in lots

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:29.360
of different ways, different philosophies
and studies. That's really important in journalism

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:33.830
to be able to look at one story through every
different angle to understand the motivation

00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:36.850
and to put yourself in other people shoes.

00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:41.780
John Miller, Head of the Dow Journalism Program
at Hillsdale, is himself an outstanding journalist

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:46.860
and still committing great journalism every
day, every week at National Review. He understands

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:51.130
what makes a story compelling, true, and important.

00:01:51.130 --> 00:01:55.530
At the heart of the program is our campus
newspaper, The Collegian. It comes out weekly

00:01:55.530 --> 00:02:01.370
during the semesters. You learn journalism
by doing journalism, by going to writing the

00:02:01.370 --> 00:02:05.370
stories, doing the interviews, laying them
out on a newspaper page, and then seeing it

00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:10.220
come out at the end of the week and watching
students read it and get their reaction.

00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:15.170
I love spending Wednesday nights in The Collegian
office. The Editor in Chief and the News

00:02:15.170 --> 00:02:20.319
Editor and I, we would work together on stuff
and we'd argue and it would be dramatic. I

00:02:20.319 --> 00:02:25.020
have really fond memories of this now because
it's a great teaching experience of basically

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:28.569
what putting out a newspaper is like, which
is what I do every day now.

00:02:28.569 --> 00:02:32.930
In a lot of ways, it's really representative
of what it takes to be successful in a really

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:38.040
meaningful career once you graduate college.
The long nights, the studying, the rigorous

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:42.010
demands that I thought almost were unfair
at the time have really helped prepare me

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:44.980
for what it took to be a journalist here,
where you have deadlines, you have multiple

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:50.120
stories a day. You're in an increasingly more
competitive journalism environment where more

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:51.020
and more is demanded of you.

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:57.490
I think one of the coolest things about the
Dow Journalism Program, the opportunity for

00:02:57.490 --> 00:03:02.420
networking with really incredible journalists.
I mean, while I was there, we had Naomi Schaefer

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:07.260
Riley come in. She spoke with our class. Talking
with her, I was able to actually pitch her an

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:11.700
idea as a student that ended up published
in The Wall Street Journal while I was still in Hillsdale

00:03:11.709 --> 00:03:19.130
It was just this amazing opportunity for me
to be faced with this group of extremely eager

00:03:19.130 --> 00:03:23.380
eighteen, nineteen, and twenty-year-olds,
how eager they were to do the work, to ask

00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:28.430
the questions that are necessary not only
to be a good journalist but to be a good citizen,

00:03:28.430 --> 00:03:30.100
to be a professional.

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:33.560
We're kind of a career development office
for people interested in the media. We work

00:03:33.569 --> 00:03:38.290
very hard to help our students land jobs upon
graduation. In fact, everybody who minors

00:03:38.290 --> 00:03:42.560
in journalism who wants a job in journalism
pretty much gets one.

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:48.530
I would much rather hire somebody who knows
something and teach them the craft, than find

00:03:48.530 --> 00:03:52.550
someone who has only learned the craft but
doesn't know anything. Journalism is a minor

00:03:52.550 --> 00:03:56.879
there, so you get to dip your toes in it,
you learn some important things, but at the

00:03:56.879 --> 00:04:04.180
same time, the emphasis remains on solid scholarship
and solid citizenship. If you learn the important

00:04:04.180 --> 00:04:09.599
stuff, it's that much easier to learn the
journalism stuff.

00:04:09.599 --> 00:04:15.770
The framers understood that journalists occupy
a special role in our republic, that's why

00:04:15.770 --> 00:04:21.560
the framers in the Bill of Rights, as the
very first amendment to the Constitution protected

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:26.250
the free press and the freedom of speech.
At Hillsdale, as part of the Dow Journalism

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:31.660
Program, they teach the Constitution. They
want young honest reporters to understand

00:04:31.660 --> 00:04:35.280
the special place that they occupy in American
society and they do a great job.

